-- title:
--   Toric–finite splitting on the ordinary corner of J_H(M)[p]
-- statement:
--   Setting. Let $p$ be a prime with $p \neq 2$, let $M$ be a nonzero natural number with $p \mid M$ and $p^2 \nmid M$, and let $H \le (\mathbb{Z}/M)^{\times}$. The hypothesis `hHp` requires that every unit $u$ whose image under `ZMod.unitsMap` for the divisibility $M/p \mid M$ is $1$ lies in $H$; $M/p$ is nonzero. A set $S$ of natural numbers is fixed, and `hin` is the predicate [`ModularCurve.HeckeDiamondInputsHAll M H`](def/ModularCurve_XHOperators.html#L113), i.e. the conjunction of `HeckeInputsHAlong (AlgebraicClosure ℚ) M H ℓ` for every prime $\ell$ together with the existence, for each $d \in (\mathbb{Z}/M)^{\times}$, of an $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-algebra automorphism $\sigma$ of $\bar F_H(M) =$ `xHFunctionFieldBar M H` satisfying `IsDiamondAutHBar M H d σ`.
--
--   Hecke data. $\mathbb{T}$ is a commutative ring which is a $\mathbb{Z}_p$-algebra acting on the Tate module [`TateModule p (JH M H)`](def/EllipticCurve_TateModule.html#L15) (the group of sequences $x$ with $p^n x_n = 0$ and $p\,x_{n+1} = x_n$, where `JH M H` is the degree-zero divisor class group $\operatorname{Pic}^0$ of $\bar F_H(M)$ over $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$) compatibly with the $\mathbb{Z}_p$-structure. The hypothesis `hfaith` says this action is faithful: an element of $\mathbb{T}$ annihilating every element of the Tate module is zero. A map `op` from the generators [`CohCarrier.Gen M S`](def/CohCarrier_Inst.html#L13) (symbols $T_\ell$ for primes $\ell \notin S$ with $\ell \nmid M$, $U_q$ for primes $q \mid M$, and diamonds $\langle d\rangle$) to $\mathbb{T}$ is given, with `hop` asserting that `op g` acts on the Tate module as [`ModularCurve.tateGenOpH M H S p g`](def/ModularCurve_XHOperators.html#L99), and `hgen` asserting that the $\mathbb{Z}_p$-subalgebra generated by the range of `op` is all of $\mathbb{T}$. Further, $S'$ is an [`IharaLemma.IdempotentSplitting`](def/IharaLemma_IdempotentSplitting.html#L7) of $\mathbb{T}$: a finite family of complete orthogonal idempotents $e_i$ together with maximal ideals $\mathfrak{m}_i$ exhausting all maximal ideals and satisfying $e_i \in \mathfrak{m}_j \iff i \neq j$; $i_0$ is an index, and `hord` is the ordinarity condition $\mathrm{op}(U_p) \notin \mathfrak{m}_{i_0}$.
--
--   Geometric data at $p$. $\mathrm{Pl}$ is a valuation subring of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ with `hPl : Pl.LiesOverPrime p`, that is $p$ lies in the nonunits of $\mathrm{Pl}$, its residue field being of characteristic $p$ and algebraically closed. The hypothesis `hj` places the $q$-expansion $j$ of the modular invariant in the function field `qExpFunctionFieldC ℚ ⊤`. $\mathfrak{X}$ is an `XHDRModelAtP p M H hpM hj`: the integral two-chart model of $X_H(M)$ over $R_p$ with its properness, flatness, integrality, finite-presentation, normality and smoothness-at-auxiliary-level clauses, a curve model $\mathfrak{X}.\mathrm{Meta}$ of $\bar F_H(M)$ over $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ with the comparison isomorphism $\mathfrak{X}.\mathrm{eeta}$ to the geometric fibre, Galois compatibility of places, $q$-expansion pinning of the finite chart, and the remaining clauses of that structure. $\Lambda$ is a `LevelData p M H hpM Pl`: a structure morphism $\sigma_A$ from $\operatorname{Spec}\mathrm{Pl}$ to the base $\operatorname{Spec}(\mathbb{Z}_{(p)}\cap\mathbb{Q})$ compatible with the geometric generic point, a scheme $\Lambda.X$ over the base with a relative group law $\Lambda.L$, a bijection $\Lambda.\mathrm{pts}$ between $J_{H'}(M/p)$, for $H'$ the image `infSubgroup p M H hpM` of $H$, and the generic points of $\Lambda.f$, and a bijection $\Lambda.\mathrm{ptsSp}$ between $\operatorname{Pic}^0$ of the level-$\Gamma_N$ function field over the residue field of $\mathrm{Pl}$ and the special-fibre points. The hypothesis `hΛ` asserts the abelian-scheme bundle for $\Lambda.f$: smoothness, properness, connected fibres, and the existence of a relative group law. Finally $O$ is a `JHNeronObjectAtP p M H hpM Pl hPl Λ`, a scheme $O.G$ over the base with relative group law $O.L$, a bijection $O.\mathrm{pts}$ from `JH M H` to its generic points, commutativity, the listed geometric properties of $O.g$, additivity and Galois equivariance of $O.\mathrm{pts}$, Hecke endomorphisms compatible with the group law and with $O.\mathrm{pts}$, flatness and surjectivity of multiplication by $n$, and the further clauses of that structure, among them the toric points and the degeneracy maps $O.\mathrm{degPts}$.
--
--   Abel–Jacobi pin. The following hypotheses, summarised here, pin the Abel–Jacobi morphism of the model. `hD` asserts that the designation built from $(O.G, O.g)$ with zero section the unit of $O.L$ represents the relative sub-Picard functor for the model morphism `toBase p (ΓM M H) hj` rigidified along $\mathfrak{X}.\varepsilon_{\inf}$, for the condition `algEquivZeroCut` (fibrewise algebraic equivalence to zero); `hDQ` asserts the same after base change to $\mathbb{Q}$, for the base-changed designation and section; `hsep` asserts that the base change to $\mathbb{Q}$ of the model morphism is separated. Further data: a morphism `ajQ` over the $\mathbb{Q}$-fibre into the base of the base-changed designation, a morphism `kQ` from the $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-fibre to the $\mathbb{Q}$-fibre of the model, a morphism `ajbar` from $\mathfrak{X}.\mathrm{Meta}.C$ to $O.G$, and a geometric point `εbar` of $\mathfrak{X}.\mathrm{Meta}.C$. The hypotheses on them are: `hpoinc`, that the Poincaré bundle of `hDQ` is isomorphic to the base change to $\mathbb{Q}$ of the Poincaré bundle of `hD` pulled back along the first projection; `hajQε`, that `ajQ` carries the base-changed section $\varepsilon_{\inf}$ to the zero section; `hajQ`, that for every field $K$, every $\mathbb{Q}$-point $t$ of $\operatorname{Spec}K$ and every $K$-point $x$ of the $\mathbb{Q}$-fibre, the pullback of the Poincaré bundle along $x$ followed by `ajQ` is isomorphic to the line bundle of the relative effective Cartier divisor of $x$ tensored with the ideal module of the divisor of the section $\varepsilon_{\inf}$ at $t$, i.e. `ajQ` realises $x \mapsto [(x) - (\infty)]$; `hkQ₁` and `hkQ₂`, the compatibility of `kQ` with the two projections, the second up to the map $\operatorname{Spec}\overline{\mathbb{Q}} \to \operatorname{Spec}\mathbb{Q}$; `hajbar`, that `ajbar` is the composite of $\mathfrak{X}.\mathrm{eeta}$, `kQ`, `ajQ` and the first projection; `hajbar_over`, that `ajbar` followed by $O.g$ is the structure morphism followed by the geometric generic point; `hεbar`, that `εbar` is the point $\infty$, i.e. its composite with $\mathfrak{X}.\mathrm{eeta}$ and the first projection is the generic point followed by $\mathfrak{X}.\varepsilon_{\inf}$; `hεbar_aj`, that `εbar` followed by `ajbar` is the generic point followed by the unit of $O.L$; `hpts_law`, that $O.\mathrm{pts}$ is additive for the relative group law obtained from `hD` through the group-theoretic cut `algEquivZeroGroupCut`; and `hAJ`, that for all geometric points $x, s$ of $\mathfrak{X}.\mathrm{Meta}.C$ with $s$ equal to $\infty$ in the above sense there is a degree-zero divisor $D_v$ on $\bar F_H(M)$ equal to $(\,\mathrm{place}(x)\,) - (\,\mathrm{place}(s)\,)$ with $O.\mathrm{pts}$ of its class given by $x$ followed by `ajbar`. The hypothesis `hrepΛ` asserts the corresponding representability statement one level up: the designation built from $(\Lambda.X, \Lambda.f)$ with zero section the unit of $\Lambda.L$ represents the relative sub-Picard functor of `toBase p (ΓN p M H hpM) hj` rigidified along $\mathfrak{X}.\varepsilon_{\inf}$ composed with $\mathfrak{X}.\pi$.
--
--   The $q$-power block. `wgen` is a semilinear automorphism of $\bar F_H(M)$ over $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$, that is a pair of ring automorphisms compatible with the structure map; `hwgen` asserts that whenever two geometric points $y, y'$ satisfy $y'$ composed with $\mathfrak{X}.\mathrm{eeta}$, the first projection and $\mathfrak{X}.w$ equals $y$ composed with $\mathfrak{X}.\mathrm{eeta}$ and the first projection, the place of $y'$ is the translate of the place of $y$ by `wgen`. Moreover $\theta$ is an $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-algebra automorphism of $\bar F_H(M)$ with `hθ`: whenever $f$ in $\bar F_H(M)$ and $u$ in $\bar F_{H'}(M/p)$ have the same Laurent expansion, the expansion of $\theta f$ is `qExpand` at $p$ of that of $u$, i.e. $q \mapsto q^p$ on expansions; and `hwθ` identifies `wgen` with the semilinear automorphism attached to $\theta$.
--
--   Degeneracy and special-fibre block. Both $\bar F_H(M)$ and $\bar F_{H'}(M/p)$ are assumed to satisfy `HasPrincipalDivisors` over $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$, namely each nonzero function has a divisor, of degree zero. The hypotheses `hΛpts_add` and `hΛptsSp_add` assert that $\Lambda.\mathrm{pts}$ is additive for $\Lambda.L$ and that $\Lambda.\mathrm{ptsSp}$ is additive for the base change of $\Lambda.L$ along the residue point, transported by `toFibrePt` and `ofFibrePt`. Two $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-algebra maps $\alpha_H, \beta_H : \bar F_{H'}(M/p) \to \bar F_H(M)$ are given, each with: integrality (`hαHint`, `hβHint`), the fundamental identity along the map (`hαHFI`, `hβHFI`), finiteness of the resulting module (`hαHfin`, `hβHfin`) and the norm formula for pushforward of divisors (`hαHN`, `hβHN`). The hypotheses `hdeg0` and `hdeg1` identify $O.\mathrm{degPts}\,0$ and $O.\mathrm{degPts}\,1$ on divisor classes with pushforward of divisors along $\alpha_H$ and along $\beta_H$ respectively. Endomorphisms $F, F^{-1}, F^{*}$ of $\operatorname{Pic}^0$ of the level-$\Gamma_N$ function field over the residue field of $\mathrm{Pl}$ are given with `hF` identifying $F$ with `qExpFrobeniusPushforwardModL`, `hFinv` asserting that $F$ and $F^{-1}$ are mutually inverse, and `hFstar` asserting $F^{*} = p\,F^{-1}$. A unit `pb` of $\mathbb{Z}/(M/p)$ with value $p$ is given, and $\delta$ is the endomorphism given by the action of the diamond automorphism `diamondActionModL` attached to the lift of `pb` to $\Gamma_0(M/p)$. Finally, additive maps $\alpha_{\mathrm{pull}}(i) : J_{H'}(M/p) \to J_H(M)$ and morphisms $\mathrm{degPull}(i)$ from $\Lambda.f$ to $O.g$, for $i \in \{0,1\}$, are given with: `hpull`, the compatibility $O.\mathrm{pts}(\alpha_{\mathrm{pull}}(i)x) = \Lambda.\mathrm{pts}(x)$ followed by $\mathrm{degPull}(i)$; `hpull_mul`, that each $\mathrm{degPull}(i)$ is a homomorphism from $\Lambda.L$ to $O.L$ on points over any base; `hpullsp`, that on the special fibre the glued $\operatorname{Pic}^0$-pair of $O.\mathrm{ptsSp}^{-1}$ of $x$ followed by $\mathrm{degPull}(i)$ equals $(x, F^{*}x)$ for $i = 0$ and $(F^{*}x, \delta x)$ for $i = 1$, where $x$ is identified through $\Lambda.\mathrm{ptsSp}^{-1}$; and `hpullα`, `hpullβ`, that $\alpha_{\mathrm{pull}}(0)$ and $\alpha_{\mathrm{pull}}(1)$ are given on classes by pullback of divisors along $\alpha_H$ and $\beta_H$ respectively, which is degree-preserving by the fundamental identity.
--
--   Conclusion. Write $X$ for the image, under the evaluation [`TateModule.proj p (JH M H) 1`](def/EllipticCurve_TateModule.html#L122) at index $1$, of the additive subgroup underlying the corner submodule $e_{i_0}\cdot \mathrm{T}_p J_H(M)$, i.e. the range of the endomorphism $e_{i_0}\cdot \mathrm{id}$ of the Tate module. Then
--   $$\#\{x \in X : x \in O.\mathrm{toricPts}\,p\}\cdot \#\{x \in X : x \in O.\mathrm{finPts}\,p\} = \#X,$$
--   the two factors being the `Set.ncard` of the indicated subsets of $J_H(M)$ and the right-hand side the `Nat.card` of the subgroup $X$. Here $O.\mathrm{toricPts}\,p$ is the subgroup generated by the range of $O.\mathrm{toricPoint}\,p$, and $O.\mathrm{finPts}\,p$ is the subgroup generated by those $x$ in the $p$-torsion of $\operatorname{Pic}^0(\bar F_H(M))$ for which `ExtendsToPlace Pl Λ.σA (O.pts x)` holds.
--
--   This is the counting step of the level-lowering argument at a prime $p$ exactly dividing the level: on the ordinary corner cut out by the idempotent $e_{i_0}$, the $p$-torsion of $J_H(M)$ factors numerically into its toric part and its finite part. It refines the corresponding global count [`ModularCurve.JHNeronObjectAtP.toricPts_le_torsion_and_finPts_le_torsion_and_natCard_mul_natCard_eq_of_representsRelSubPicLevel`](thm.html#ModularCurve.JHNeronObjectAtP.toricPts_le_torsion_and_finPts_le_torsion_and_natCard_mul_natCard_eq_of_representsRelSubPicLevel), from which it is deduced together with the equivariance of the idempotent projector for the Hecke generators, and it feeds the criterion identifying when the residual representation attached to a point of the corner is reducible or has cyclotomic inertia at $p$.
